# Planner Positions

You are evaluating proposed amendments to this project. Assess each amendment on its technical merit — correctness, safety, robustness, and maintainability.

## Instructions

1. Consider each amendment carefully on its own merits.
2. If planner positions are provided below, weigh them as additional context when forming your decision.
3. Vote ACCEPT or REJECT for each amendment with a clear rationale.
4. Do NOT reject amendments because they are "out of scope" or "beyond the original spec" — any real bug or safety issue is valid regardless of scope.
5. Do NOT dismiss concurrency or isolation issues as "theoretical" just because they don't currently cause failures — shared mutable state is a defect even if current callers happen to be read-only.

## Proposed Amendments

## Amendment: fr-6-ea5e8020

In calibration.rs, `classify_f64` does not validate that `min <= max`. An inverted range (min > max) causes ALL values to silently classify as Fail because the bounds check `value < min || value > max` is satisfied for every input when the range is inverted. Add a `debug_assert!(min <= max)` at the top of `classify_f64` (and by extension `classify_u32`) to catch this class of bug during testing. The Envelope builder methods currently pass min/max in the correct order, so this is a latent bug, not a live one — but the calibration module is public API meant for external use, and an inverted range would produce confusing all-fail results with no diagnostic.

## Amendment: fr-6-1e95ff10

In calibration.rs `CalibrationRun::finish()`, when `overall_status != CheckStatus::Pass`, artifacts are written twice: first via `self.inner.persist_artifacts()` on line 338, then again inside `self.inner.finish()` on line 345 (because `check_envelope` records failures in the inner harness via `record_failure`, so `finish()` sees non-empty failures and calls `write_artifacts` again). The second write overwrites the first. Consider either (a) not calling `persist_artifacts` separately and letting `finish` handle it, or (b) calling a consume-without-artifacts method if one exists. This is a minor inefficiency, not a correctness bug.

## Amendment: fr-6-cd69e379

In calibration.rs lines 76-77, the doc comment "Result of checking a single field against its envelope bounds." is duplicated verbatim on consecutive lines above the `FieldCheck` struct. Remove the duplicate line.

## Amendment: fr-6-ff6cd856

The patch is functionally unrelated to the requested shrimp-scenario bead. The only added integration tests are nitrification/alkalinity checks at crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs:63, :107, :147, :205, :243, and :278, and the code changes in crates/tank_core/src/engine.rs:229, crates/tank_core/src/systems/nitrogen_cycle.rs:12, crates/tank_core/src/systems/water_change.rs:131, and crates/tank_core/src/budget_helpers.rs:66 are all alkalinity-accounting plumbing. None of the required shrimp scenarios (breeding success, thermal suppression, chemistry stress, chloride protection, crash mode), per-stage shrimp/water dumps, or the required e2e runner/report are present, so this does not satisfy the bead’s acceptance criteria.

## Amendment: fr-6-c5e01eba

The only new test file, crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs:1, is still untracked. `git diff` contains only the src/ changes, so unless this file is explicitly added before submission, the new alkalinity-budget logic will land without any of the accompanying regression coverage.

